    "plain_text": "Alberto Cortina de Alcocer entered Construcciones y Contratas after marrying Alicia Koplowitz, whose family owned the business. Working with his cousin Alberto Alcocer, he became chief executive and helped diversify the builder into a much larger corporate group. After the cousins left FCC, they shifted towards banking and investments, jointly controlling Banco Zaragozano before selling their stake to Barclays in 2004.\n\nCortina’s current wealth is anchored in construction and infrastructure investments. Through Percacer and jointly controlled holding companies, he and Alcocer retain a substantial voting block in ACS. A 2026 agreement coordinates their votes to support stable shareholder decisions. That structure makes Cortina an investor and active voting principal rather than an operating executive of ACS, while his earlier career remains defined by construction-company management and banking.",
